{"type":"assistant","uuid":"868a5741-fe25-4df1-ab73-d6ffcda8b72b","session_id":"fab1830e-aa74-488e-8a22-42b2dc0b9084","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"868a5741-fe25-4df1-ab73-d6ffcda8b72b","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash:0731-cloud","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The connection_assignments parameter expects \"unique current server connection IDs\" â€” likely the `choice_id` values from the serial_choices list, which are the stable_usb_identity values. I passed those first (066FFF514988525067233337 etc.) and got the same error. Hmm, wait â€” the first attempt used the stable_usb_identity values and got the same error. Let me re-check: first attempt used `066FFF514988525067233337` and `0668FF514988525067213913` â€” those ARE the choice_ids. And it failed with \"connection assignments must be unique current server connection IDs\".\n\nMaybe the connection_assignments expects the *friendly connection* IDs from a different inventory â€” perhaps the debug probe connections (not serial). The `connections` field was empty in the response. The serial_choices are UART ports. The server may distinguish \"connections\" (debug probes) from \"serial choices\" (UART). Since `connections: []`, there are no current server connections to assign â€” the probes are visible via serial enumeration but not yet connected as debug sessions.\n\nThe error says assignments must be \"unique current server connection IDs\" â€” with `connections: []`, there are no current connection IDs, so any assignment fails. The server wants me to clarify which boards are attached â€” perhaps by passing only the board names and letting the server route, or by passing the choice_id values in a different field.\n\nActually, re-reading the setup_overview description: \"connection_assignments\" â€” \"Other visible debug probes may remain unassigned.\" The parameter is for assigning *debug probe connections* to board names. Since `connections: []` (no debug connections currently), there's nothing to assign.\n\nThe serial_choices are UART ports, not debug connections. The server's inventory distinguishes them. So the correct approach: the server says \"There are more requested board names than visible debug connections\" â€” meaning it sees 0 debug connections but 2 requested boards. The serial choices (COM12/COM17) are the STM probes' VCOM UARTs, which are visible, but the debug connections aren't established yet.\n\nHmm, but the spec says \"Exact live inventory must confirm them\" â€” the serial inventory does confirm both STM probe identities (COM12 ending 233337, COM17 ending 213913) matching the assignment. The J-Link ports (COM15/COM16) are the Nordic boards.\n\nThe server wants clarification of which requested boards are currently attached. Since the serial inventory confirms both STM probes are present, and the resource assignment names them, I should pass the board names with the serial choice mapping. But the connection_assignments field rejected both stable_usb_identity and port_path values.\n\nMaybe the field expects the choice_id from serial_choices â€” which IS the stable_usb_identity. That failed. Or maybe it expects a different format. Let me try passing connection_assignments with the choice_id values exactly as returned: \"0668FF514988525067213913\" and \"066FFF514988525067233337\" â€” that was my first attempt, which failed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should pass only the two STM board names and let the server route them against the serial inventory. The error message \"There are more requested board names than visible debug connections\" suggests the server counts debug connections, not serial ports. With connections: [], it can't route.\n\nPerhaps the server expects me to first establish which probes are attached via a different mechanism. The handshake says: \"At startup, ask the user in ordinary conversation for one unique, familiar name for each board they want to use in this project now, or 'no board'