er wants me to first establish which probes are connected â€” perhaps via a probe enumeration that happens during setup.\n\nThe setup flow per the handshake: \"Route an unknown name to setup through the all-NULL board_setup-plan first; ask for the exact package-level MCU part number and the authoritative local datasheet PDF... then load board_setup-plan and submit its populated plan before any other hardware plan.\"\n\nSo for unknown names (no profiles exist â€” profiles: []), the route is board_setup-plan. The setup_overview returned status \"setup_assignment_clarification_required\" with no routes. The server wants clarification of which boards are attached.\n\nGiven the serial inventory confirms both STM probes, and the resource assignment names them, the clarification is: both STM-A and STM-B are attached (their VCOMs COM12/COM17 are enumerated). The J-Link ports belong to Nordic boards not owned by this test.\n\nPerhaps I should call setup_overview with just the two names and no connection_assignments, but the server already did that and asked for clarification. The clarification mechanism might be to pass connection_assignments mapping names to the serial choice_ids â€” which I did and it failed with \"must be unique current server connection IDs\".\n\nHmm, let me think about what \"current server connection IDs\" means. Maybe it's the choice_id from serial_choices â€” those ARE current server connection IDs for the serial ports. My first attempt passed exactly those values: \"066FFF514988525067233337\" for STM-A and \"0668FF514988525067213913\" for STM-B. That failed.\n\nUnless the values need to be the full choice objects or the port_path. I tried port_path too.\n\nWait â€” maybe the parameter expects the mapping in the reverse direction or a different key format. Or maybe the server wants me to pass connection_assignments as a list of choice_ids, not a dict. The schema says \"connection_assignments\": {\"type\": \"object\"} â€” a dict.\n\nLet me look at the